+# Bidirectional Search (experimental)
+
+*Since pymsbwt 0.5.0.*  **Experimental**: the API and the on-disk companion
+format may change before a production release.  It exists to prove that
+bidirectional search is exact on msBWT indexes; it is not yet faster than
+the existing `extendRight`, and it has no command-line tool.
+
+An ordinary msBWT extends a match to the left cheaply.  Extending to the right
+needs a second view of the data.  `MUS.Bidirectional` supports two:
+
+| Design | What is built | Counts |
+|---|---|---|
+| 2BWT companion | a second index of the **reversed** reads, next to your index | single strand, same as today |
+| FMD index | one index of the reads **plus their reverse complements** | both strands, always |
+
+Your existing index is never modified.
+
+## 2BWT companion
+
+```python
+from MUS import Bidirectional as bd
+
+bd.build_reverse_companion("data/merged10", "data/merged10.reverse")
+index = bd.BidirectionalIndex.load("data/merged10", "data/merged10.reverse")
+
+state = index.search("ACGT")             # BiInterval(forward, reverse, size)
+state = index.extend_right(state, "A")   # ACGTA
+state = index.extend_left(state, "T")    # TACGTA
+print(state.size)                        # occurrences of TACGTA
+
+children = index.extend_right_all(state) # {b"A": BiInterval, b"C": ..., ...}
+```
+
+`state.forward` / `state.size` is an ordinary interval of your index, so the
+per-sample queries apply to it, for example
+`MultiSourceQueryIndex.count_interval(source, state.forward, state.forward + state.size)`.
+
+## FMD index
+
+```python
+bd.build_fmd_index("data/merged10", "data/merged10.fmd")
+fmd = bd.BidirectionalIndex.load_fmd("data/merged10.fmd")
+fmd.search("ACGT").size    # occurrences of ACGT on either strand
+```
+
+An FMD index carries no source provenance and always counts both strands.
+It is a screen for a possible future index type, not a replacement for your
+index.
+
+## Building
+
+- Construction recovers every read from your index and builds a new one, so
+  it takes about as long as the original build and holds the reads in memory.
+- Call it from a script file or a notebook, not from `python -` reading
+  stdin: index construction uses `multiprocessing`, and Python 3.14 starts
+  worker processes in a way that needs an importable main script.
+- `reverse_companion.json` / `fmd_index.json` bind the new index to the exact
+  `msbwt.npy` it was built from; a stale companion is rejected on load.
+
+## Status
+
+Correctness was checked against direct search for every extension order of
+every pattern up to length 3, every read substring, palindromes, `N`,
+duplicate reads, read ends, and per-sample counts.  On synthetic data, an FMD
+index had 15–21% fewer BWT runs than a 2BWT at the same size, which matters
+only once a run-length compressed backend exists.  The design discussion is
+in `docs/roadmap/CHECKPOINT_FMD_VS_2BWT.md` in the main repository.
+
+## Related pages
+
+- [Source-Aware and Multi-Source Queries](Source-Aware-and-Multi-Source-Queries)
+- [Persistent Files and Compatibility](Persistent-Files-and-Compatibility)
