@@ -0,0 +1,96 @@
+# LCP Intervals and Maximal Repeats
+
+*Since pymsbwt 0.5.0 (Feature 11C).*  Requires an LCP layer
+([LCP Retrofit](LCP-Retrofit)) and a provenance-preserving merged index.
+
+An **LCP interval** is a block of BWT rows whose suffixes all start with the
+same string, and which is as large as possible for that string.  These are the
+internal nodes of the suffix tree of your reads.  Because an LCP interval is
+an ordinary BWT interval, the per-sample queries from
+[Source-Aware and Multi-Source Queries](Source-Aware-and-Multi-Source-Queries)
+work on it directly: a repeat reports which samples contain it without
+listing every occurrence.
+
+## Definitions
+
+Each read end acts as its own terminator, so a match never runs across reads.
+
+| Term | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| maximal repeat | occurs at least twice and cannot be extended to the left or right without losing an occurrence |
+| supermaximal repeat | a maximal repeat that is not part of a longer maximal repeat |
+
+## Command line
+
+```bash
+# maximal repeats of length >= 25 found in at least 3 samples
+msbwt-lcp repeats --input data/merged10 --min-length 25 --min-sources 3 \
+    --include-sources
+
+# supermaximal repeats only, first 100
+msbwt-lcp repeats --input data/merged10 --min-length 20 --supermaximal --limit 100
+
+# counts for a metadata group, keeping repeats seen at least 5 times in it
+msbwt-lcp repeats --input data/merged10 --min-length 25 --group case --min-selected 5
+```
+
+Output is one JSON record per line:
+
+```json
+{"end": 1042, "length": 31, "occurrences": 12, "sequence": "ACGT...", "source_frequency": 4, "start": 1030}
+```
+
+Other options: `--max-length`, `--min-occurrences`, `--max-sources`,
+`--include-read-count` (needs read provenance).
+
+## Python API
+
+```python
+from MUS.MultiSourceQuery import MultiSourceBWT
+
+msbwt = MultiSourceBWT.load("data/merged10")      # loads lcps.npy automatically
+
+for record in msbwt.maximalRepeats(min_length=25, min_sources=3,
+                                   include_sources=True, limit=10):
+    print(record["sequence"], record["occurrences"], record["source_frequency"])
+    for source in record["sources"]:
+        print("   ", source["source_name"], source["count"])
+
+# Raw intervals (suffix-tree internal nodes), children first:
+for interval in msbwt.lcpIntervals(min_lcp=30):
+    print(interval.lcp, interval.start, interval.end,
+          msbwt.lcpIntervalSequence(interval))
+    children = msbwt.lcpChildren(interval)   # [(start, end, lcp or None), ...]
+    break
+```
+
+`maximalRepeats` records always contain `start`, `end`, `length`,
+`occurrences` and `source_frequency`.  Optional fields:
+
+| Option | Adds |
+|---|---|
+| `include_sequence=True` (default) | `sequence` (bytes) |
+| `include_sources=True` | `sources`: per-sample counts, nonzero samples only |
+| `sources=` / `group=` / `where=` | `selected_count` for that selection; filter with `min_selected` |
+| `include_read_count=True` | `read_count`: distinct reads containing the repeat (needs read provenance) |
+
+## Cost
+
+Enumeration is one pass over `lcps.npy`.  Rows below `--min-length` are
+skipped quickly, so a higher minimum length is much faster: on a synthetic
+10M-row array, minimum length 1 took about 8 s and 25 took about 0.1 s.
+Each reported repeat costs one walk along a single read to spell it, plus one
+provenance-tree traversal for the sample counts.
+
+## Notes
+
+- Works on any valid LCP layer, including one carried through
+  [Source Removal](Source-Removal).
+- Nothing new is written to the index directory.
+- Repeats containing `N` are reported like any other symbol.
+
+## Related pages
+
+- [LCP Retrofit](LCP-Retrofit)
+- [Source-Aware and Multi-Source Queries](Source-Aware-and-Multi-Source-Queries)
+- [Read-Level Provenance](Read-Level-Provenance)
