 ## Interaction with source removal
 
-Removing sources drops the LCP layer unless explicitly handled:
+*Since pymsbwt 0.5.0 (Feature 11B).*  Removing sources keeps the LCP
+layer.  When removal makes two surviving rows adjacent, their LCP is the
+minimum of the original LCPs between them, so the reduced layer is computed
+in one sequential pass with no FASTQ and no read recovery.  It is exactly
+the array a fresh `msbwt-lcp construct` would produce on the reduced index.
 
 ```bash
 msbwt-remove-sources --input data/merged10 --output data/merged7 \
-    --remove sample00,sample02 --drop-lcp
+    --remove sample00,sample02            # LCP carried through
+msbwt-remove-sources --input data/merged10 --output data/merged7 \
+    --remove sample00,sample02 --drop-lcp # reduced index without LCP
 ```
 
-LCP-preserving removal is **not** part of this release.
+The input layer is validated first; a stale layer stops the removal and
+nothing is written.  The reduced `lcp.json` records
+`"algorithm": "point10-survivor-range-minimum"`, the input digest under
+`derived_from`, and `"max_read_length_exact": false` (the inherited value
+stays a valid upper bound).
+
+In pymsbwt 0.4.0 and in the frozen Python 2.7 line, removal from an
+LCP-enabled index fails unless `--drop-lcp` is given.
+
+## Using the LCP layer
+
+LCP intervals, maximal repeats and per-sample repeat support are described
+on [LCP Intervals and Maximal Repeats](LCP-Intervals-and-Maximal-Repeats).
 
-## Future / not included
